Git stores all data as objects identified by their hash inside .git/objects.

Every file you add becomes a blob object stored under that directory.

GitHub itself uses a large‑scale infrastructure (“Spokes”) to store these Git objects on their servers.

So the answer is:

Every file you create or upload is stored as a Git object (a blob) inside the repository’s .git/objects database on GitHub’s backend.

🧩 How It Works (Simplified)
